/// \defgroup grp_w1019_apt_ui_callback_firers Apt HUD callback firers
/// \ingroup grp_gui_apt
/// \brief Small free functions that push battle HUD state changes from the
///        engine into an Apt movie by calling one named ActionScript
///        function each, with at most one string argument.
///
/// The Apt screens and HUD work in two directions. The movie calls native
/// code through callbacks that controllers register by name
/// (\ref sub_gui_apt_screens). The functions here go the other way: native
/// code calls a function by name in a loaded movie. Each firer passes one
/// fixed function name and, at most, one fixed or formatted argument
/// string; the movie's ActionScript decides what that does on screen.
///
/// \par How a firer reaches the movie
/// - Every firer calls one shared invoke helper with the same arguments in
///   the same order: the movie level stored by setUiCallbackOwner(), the
///   function name, an argument count of 1 or 0, the argument string (a
///   null pointer when the count is 0), and four further arguments that are
///   always zero, so the helper always receives eight arguments. It is
///   called on a global object, but its body does not use that object.
/// - The helper rejects any level of 14 or more, compared as an unsigned
///   value. For a lower level it builds the path `/_levelN` (N being the
///   level number) and hands the function name, a static result buffer,
///   that path, the argument count and the arguments to a further entry
///   point. That this entry point belongs to the Apt library and runs the
///   named ActionScript function on that level is inferred from the
///   argument shape and the strings, not from its own body.
/// - The helper returns its result buffer. Every firer here discards it, so
///   these calls are fire-and-forget.
/// - In retail the stored level starts at -1 (all bits set) in the data
///   section. Read as unsigned, that is above the limit, so a firer called
///   before setUiCallbackOwner() does nothing.
///
/// \par Which movie
/// setUiCallbackOwner() has one caller, the constructor of the object that
/// owns the palantir. It loads a movie from the `Apt` folder through the
/// same global object that runs the invoke helper, passes the returned level
/// to setUiCallbackOwner(), and passes the same level to AptPalantir's
/// constructor. The firers therefore target the palantir's movie. The load
/// also takes a second string from a global; which movie file the load
/// selects is not established from this code. BFME 1: Open-BFME-1's
/// donor unit for the same pattern (Q2NamedScriptCallbacks.cpp) passes
/// the AptPalantir window index as the level, and fires
/// `SetMovieButtonState`, `FadePalantirButtons` and
/// `SetObserverStuffState` with the same argument strings as BFME 2. That
/// unit also fires `SetSelectAllHeroesButtonState` (`_up` and `_unused`).
/// BFME 2 sends that callback with the same two strings, but not through
/// these firers: it goes through a different Apt call helper that takes a
/// level and a prefix string, and
/// InGameHeroSelectInterface::Impl::ShowSelectAllHeroesBttn is the sender
/// of `_up`.
///
/// \par How callers use them
/// - The state firers (the show/hide, fade, flash, enable/highlight,
///   set...State and progress calls) are sent by their callers only when
///   the caller's cached value changes (setPlayerButtonsState() is also
///   sent the first time), so the movie holds the current state. Each
///   function below names the callers checked for it.
/// - setHideScroll() is not cached: AptPalantir::update sends it when a
///   pending flag is set, and a setter method of the palantir's owner
///   object (the object whose constructor calls setUiCallbackOwner())
///   sends it on every call, even with an unchanged value. That setter is
///   reached from map-script action handling; see setHideScroll().
/// - The effect firers (the Play...Effect calls) and hideSpellBook() are
///   one-shot events.
/// - To list the current callers of any of these functions, find the
///   function's row in `reverse/functions.csv` (`rg` for its name) and pass
///   the address from that row to `tools/callers_of.py`. The tool takes an
///   address, not a name, and prints each function that calls it directly
///   under its current ledger name; callers still held as anonymous dumps
///   are not listed.
///
/// \par The functions and what they send
/// | ActionScript function | Firer | Argument |
/// |---|---|---|
/// | `SetMovieButtonState` | showMovieButton(), hideMovieButton() | `_show` or `_hide` |
/// | `FadePalantirButtons` | fadePalantirButtonsZero(), fadePalantirButtonsOne() | `0` or `1` |
/// | `SetObserverStuffState` | showObserverStuff(), hideObserverStuff() | `_show` or `_hide` |
/// | `SetResourceIconState` | setResourceIconState() | `_show` (true) or `_hide` (false) |
/// | `FlashObjectivesButton` | setFlashObjectivesButton() | `_show` (true) or `_hide` (false) |
/// | `EnablePlayerMagicButton` | enablePlayerMagicButton() | `1` (true) or `0` (false) |
/// | `HighlightPlayerMagicButton` | highlightPlayerMagicButton() | `1` (true) or `0` (false) |
/// | `SetPlayerButtonsState` | setPlayerButtonsState() | `_ring` (true) or `_evenstar` (false) |
/// | `SetPlayerPowerCapState` | setPlayerPowerCapState() | `_ring` (true) or `_evenstar` (false) |
/// | `SetPalantirFrameState` | setPalantirFrameState() | `_hide`, `_good`, `_goodSingle`, `_evil` or `_evilSingle` for 0 to 4 |
/// | `SetPlayerMagicProgress` | setPlayerMagicProgress() | the value as decimal text |
/// | `HideScroll` | setHideScroll() | `0` or `1`, from a query on the palantir's owner object |
/// | `HideSpellBook` | hideSpellBook() | none |
/// | `PlayCommandPointEffect` | playCommandPointEffect() | none |
/// | `PlayPlayerSpellPointEffect` | playPlayerSpellPointEffect() | none |
/// | `PlayPlayerLevelUpEffect` | playPlayerLevelUpEffect() | none |
///
/// The table covers the functions documented in this group only. Other
/// native code also calls the same invoke helper with the same stored
/// level, so it targets the same movie, with further function names. That
/// includes `SetPlayerFaction` and the button-flash calls
/// (`CreateButtonFlash`, `ShowButtonFlash`, `MoveButtonFlash`,
/// `DeleteButtonFlash` and `HideButtonFlash`), which pass a name string
/// and, for `MoveButtonFlash`, two numbers formatted as text.
/// AptPalantir::update also sends `SetMessengerButtonState` to the
/// palantir's own level (the one its constructor received) through another
/// small helper.
///
/// Callers of the invoke helper that do not use the stored level pass
/// other levels. Some pass a fixed number: the senders of `HideEndGame`
/// that were checked pass 13. Others take the level from the calling
/// object, or from an argument of a small wrapper function. This page
/// does not survey those callers.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: what each call does on screen. The movie's ActionScript
///   defines these functions and decides how it shows each argument value,
///   so a mod can restyle or re-animate any of these responses in the
///   movie.
/// - Data-driven, for one firer: map-script action handling can set or
///   clear the flag that setHideScroll() queries, and each such call
///   sends `HideScroll` at once. Which script action does this is not
///   established; see setHideScroll().
/// - Hard-coded: the function names, every argument string, the order of
///   the five frame states, and, apart from the script path to
///   setHideScroll(), when the engine calls each firer. For the HUD to
///   react to these calls, a replacement palantir movie needs functions
///   with the names in the table that handle these exact strings, and
///   likewise the other functions sent to the same level, which include
///   those listed above (`SetPlayerFaction`, the button-flash calls and
///   `SetMessengerButtonState`). What the Apt runtime does when a called
///   function is missing from the movie is not established here. These
///   firers only ever send the listed names and values; pushing new HUD
///   state into the movie this way needs a new native firer or caller.
/// - Lockstep: the firers only pass strings to the movie and ignore the
///   result. Apart from the query in setHideScroll(), they read no game
///   state, and they change none themselves.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - These functions are a narrow seam between the engine and the HUD:
///   each is one call with a function name and at most one ASCII string. A
///   replacement HUD can implement the same entry points, or the shared
///   helper, without touching the callers.
/// - The helper only accepts levels below 14, and setPlayerMagicProgress()
///   formats into a four-byte buffer; both limits matter if the HUD is
///   rehosted or its ranges widened.
///
/// \see \ref sub_gui_apt


/// \file UiCallbackFirers.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1019_apt_ui_callback_firers
/// \brief Native-to-movie firers for the battle HUD, and the setter for the
///        movie level they target.
///
/// The firers documented in this group share one shape: each calls the
/// shared invoke helper with the stored level, one ActionScript function
/// name and at most one argument string (a null pointer when there is
/// none). See \ref grp_w1019_apt_ui_callback_firers for the call contract.


/// \fn void setUiCallbackOwner(void *owner)
/// \brief Stores the Apt movie level that every firer in this group
///        targets.
///
/// The value is kept in one global and passed, unchanged, as the first
/// argument of every firer's call to the shared invoke helper, which reads
/// it as a movie level number.
///
/// Its only caller is the constructor of the object that owns the palantir.
/// It loads a movie from the `Apt` folder through the same global object
/// that runs the invoke helper, passes the returned level here (cast to a
/// pointer), and passes the same level to AptPalantir's constructor. So the
/// firers target the palantir's movie.
///
/// In retail the stored level starts at -1 (all bits set) in the data
/// section. Read as unsigned, that is above the limit, so until this runs
/// the helper rejects the level and the firers do nothing.
///
/// \param owner The movie level, stored as a pointer-sized value. It is not
///              a pointer and is never dereferenced; the helper ignores
///              values of 14 or more (unsigned).
///
/// \see showMovieButton(), setPalantirFrameState()


/// \fn void showMovieButton()
/// \brief Calls `SetMovieButtonState` with `_show`.
///
/// AptPalantir::update calls it, together with fadePalantirButtonsZero(),
/// when its movie playback state turns to showing; it calls the pair
/// hideMovieButton() and fadePalantirButtonsOne() when it turns back. The
/// caller sends only on a change.
///
/// \see hideMovieButton(), fadePalantirButtonsZero()


/// \fn void hideMovieButton()
/// \brief Calls `SetMovieButtonState` with `_hide`.
///
/// The counterpart of showMovieButton(); AptPalantir::update pairs it with
/// fadePalantirButtonsOne().
///
/// \see showMovieButton(), fadePalantirButtonsOne()


/// \fn void fadePalantirButtonsZero()
/// \brief Calls `FadePalantirButtons` with the string `0`.
///
/// AptPalantir::update sends it with showMovieButton(). What `0` means to
/// the movie (faded out or not faded) is defined by the movie, not by this
/// code.
///
/// \see fadePalantirButtonsOne(), showMovieButton()


/// \fn void fadePalantirButtonsOne()
/// \brief Calls `FadePalantirButtons` with the string `1`.
///
/// AptPalantir::update sends it with hideMovieButton().
///
/// \see fadePalantirButtonsZero(), hideMovieButton()


/// \fn void showObserverStuff()
/// \brief Calls `SetObserverStuffState` with `_show`.
///
/// AptPalantir::update tracks a condition built from a game-logic test and
/// a player-list test (presumably whether the local player is observing,
/// going by the callback name and the observer buttons under
/// `ObserverStuff`) and sends showObserverStuff() or hideObserverStuff()
/// when it changes. The palantir's observer
/// next-player and previous-player buttons sit under the movie's
/// `ObserverStuff` clip.
///
/// \see hideObserverStuff()


/// \fn void hideObserverStuff()
/// \brief Calls `SetObserverStuffState` with `_hide`.
///
/// \see showObserverStuff()


/// \fn void setResourceIconState(bool show)
/// \brief Shows or hides the HUD's resource icon by calling
///        `SetResourceIconState`.
///
/// The caller is the update method of the palantir's resource-display
/// helper, which AptPalantir::update calls during its own. It passes whether
/// it found a resource amount to show for the local player, and only when
/// that changes.
///
/// \param show true sends `_show`, false sends `_hide`.
///
/// \see playCommandPointEffect()


/// \fn void setFlashObjectivesButton(bool show)
/// \brief Sends a flash flag to the movie's `FlashObjectivesButton`
///        function.
///
/// AptPalantir::update keeps a flash flag. It is set when a per-index
/// status flag goes from clear to set in a record list that the caller
/// polls through a global. That these records are the mission objectives
/// is presumed from the callback name only. The flag is cleared later
/// under conditions the caller tests. This firer sends the flag each time
/// it changes.
///
/// \param show true sends `_show`, false sends `_hide`.


/// \fn void enablePlayerMagicButton(bool enable)
/// \brief Enables or disables the player magic button by calling
///        `EnablePlayerMagicButton`.
///
/// Called by Palantir::Impl::UpdatePlayerStats when the enabled state it
/// tracks changes.
///
/// \param enable true sends `1`, false sends `0`.
///
/// \see highlightPlayerMagicButton(), setPlayerMagicProgress()


/// \fn void highlightPlayerMagicButton(bool highlight)
/// \brief Turns the player magic button's highlight on or off by calling
///        `HighlightPlayerMagicButton`.
///
/// Palantir::Impl::UpdatePlayerStats sets its highlight flag when one of
/// twenty control bar entries it polls for the local player becomes
/// available, clears it under conditions it tests later, and sends the
/// flag through this firer when it changes.
///
/// \param highlight true sends `1`, false sends `0`.
///
/// \see enablePlayerMagicButton()


/// \fn void setPlayerButtonsState(bool ring)
/// \brief Switches the player buttons between their two styles by calling
///        `SetPlayerButtonsState`.
///
/// Palantir::Impl::UpdatePlayerStats passes a flag from the local player's
/// player template, the first time and whenever it changes; at such a
/// change it also resets the magic-button state it tracks.
///
/// \param ring true sends `_ring`, false sends `_evenstar`.
///
/// \see setPlayerPowerCapState()


/// \fn void setHideScroll()
/// \brief Calls `HideScroll` with `0` or `1`, chosen by a query on the
///        palantir's owner object.
///
/// The firer reads the global that holds the palantir's owner object: the
/// object whose constructor calls setUiCallbackOwner() and creates the
/// AptPalantir (see \ref grp_w1019_apt_ui_callback_firers, "Which movie").
/// The factory that fills this global constructs an object whose base-class
/// constructor is that one. The firer calls a query method on the owner.
/// The query is true when a flag on the Living World singleton
/// (TheLivingWorld) is clear and a flag in an object the owner holds is
/// set. True sends `0`, false sends `1`. What the two flags mean is not
/// established.
///
/// By layout, the object the query reads through is probably the
/// AptPalantir itself. The owner's constructor stores the new AptPalantir
/// in an owned pointer slot, and the query reads through a pointer at the
/// same offset. The queried flag then sits next to the pending flag that
/// AptPalantir::update tests (below), and the AptPalantir constructor sets
/// both. This rests on field offsets that agree across separately
/// reconstructed views of the two classes, not on a named field.
///
/// Callers:
/// - AptPalantir::update, when a pending flag is set: it calls the radar's
///   newMap, then this firer, then clears the flag. The palantir's
///   constructor sets that flag.
/// - A setter method of the palantir's owner object, which writes the
///   queried flag and then calls this firer on every call, even when the
///   value does not change. The setter is reached from map-script action
///   handling: ScriptActions::executeAction dispatches two small handlers
///   that call it, one that sets the flag and one that clears it. Which
///   script action they implement is not established.
/// - Another method of the palantir's owner object, which calls this firer
///   and then hideSpellBook(). That method's caller is a method of the
///   LivingWorld class.
///
/// \par Modding
/// A map script that runs the action behind those handlers sets or clears
/// the queried flag and sends `HideScroll` straight away, so the timing of
/// this call is partly script-driven. The strings `0` and `1`, and the
/// test that picks between them, are fixed in code.
///
/// \see hideSpellBook()


/// \fn void hideSpellBook()
/// \brief Calls `HideSpellBook` with no arguments.
///
/// Its one caller is a method of the palantir's owner object, the same
/// object that setHideScroll() queries. That method calls setHideScroll()
/// first and then this firer, and is itself called by a method of the
/// LivingWorld class.
///
/// \see setHideScroll()


/// \fn void playCommandPointEffect()
/// \brief Calls `PlayCommandPointEffect` with no arguments.
///
/// The same resource-display helper that calls setResourceIconState() calls
/// it when the start delay of one of its animated counter changes runs
/// out.
///
/// \see setResourceIconState()


/// \fn void playPlayerSpellPointEffect()
/// \brief Calls `PlayPlayerSpellPointEffect` with no arguments.
///
/// AptPalantir::update keeps a pending record that holds a countdown and an
/// amount. On each update that reaches this code, it decrements the
/// countdown. If the countdown is still above zero, it subtracts the amount
/// from a points value that it reads from the Living World logic object,
/// before passing that value on. Once the countdown reaches zero, it
/// deletes the record and calls this firer instead. That the record
/// concerns spell points is presumed from the callback name only.
/// That code runs only when the Living World logic object exists and one
/// of its state tests passes.
///
/// \see playPlayerLevelUpEffect()


/// \fn void playPlayerLevelUpEffect()
/// \brief Calls `PlayPlayerLevelUpEffect` with no arguments.
///
/// Palantir::Impl::UpdatePlayerStats calls it when the local player's
/// level changes, but only when the level it recorded before is not
/// negative and the new level is higher; a drop never fires it.
///
/// \see setPlayerMagicProgress()


/// \fn void setPalantirFrameState(int state)
/// \brief Sets the palantir frame's look by calling `SetPalantirFrameState`
///        with one of five state names.
///
/// On each update that reaches its HUD work, AptPalantir::update computes
/// the frame state; when it differs from the last one sent, it calls this
/// firer, resets several of the cached states it compares against, and
/// skips the rest of its HUD work for that update.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The state indexes a fixed five-entry table of names, in this order:
/// `_hide`, `_good`, `_goodSingle`, `_evil`, `_evilSingle`.
///
/// \param state Index into that table, 0 to 4.
///
/// \warning Retail has no bounds check: a value outside 0 to 4 reads
///          outside the table. The WorldBuilder build's twin, compiled from
///          AptPalantir.cpp, asserts that the state is at least 0 and below
///          the frame-state count.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The five names and their order are fixed in code. For the frame to
/// change with each state, a palantir movie needs to handle all five
/// strings; a new frame look needs both a new name here and a caller that
/// sends it.


/// \fn void setPlayerPowerCapState(bool ring)
/// \brief Switches the player power-cap display between its two styles by
///        calling `SetPlayerPowerCapState`.
///
/// AptPalantir::update calls it on a change, with a flag it reads from the
/// campaign manager. That code runs only when the Living World logic
/// object exists and one of its state tests passes.
///
/// \param ring true sends `_ring`, false sends `_evenstar`.
///
/// \see setPlayerButtonsState()


/// \fn void setPlayerMagicProgress(int progress)
/// \brief Sends a progress value to the movie as decimal text through
///        `SetPlayerMagicProgress`.
///
/// Palantir::Impl::UpdatePlayerStats computes a progress value for the
/// local player and calls this firer whenever the value changes.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - A range guard is meant to reject values outside 1 to 100, but it is
///   written as "less than 1 and greater than 100", which no value
///   satisfies. Retail keeps that test, so every value is sent. The
///   WorldBuilder build's twin, compiled from AptPalantir.cpp, carries the
///   same condition in an assert with the message "Invalid player magic
///   progress specified".
/// - The value is formatted with sprintf into the storage of the argument
///   itself, four bytes, and that text is passed as the argument. The
///   WorldBuilder twin formats into a four-byte local buffer instead.
///
/// \param progress The value to send. The caller computes it for the local
///                 player; the intended range, from the guard, is 1 to
///                 100.
///
/// \warning The text buffer holds three characters and the terminator.
///          Values from -99 to 999 fit; longer text overruns the argument
///          slot. Callers must keep the value in range, since the guard
///          does not.
///
/// \see enablePlayerMagicButton(), playPlayerLevelUpEffect()
